<section xml:id="notes-content-moderation">
  <title>Algorithmic Governance and Content Moderation</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Content moderation is a governance problem at massive scale.
      Platforms, regulators, and oversight bodies decide what speech is allowed, what is removed, and how automated systems contribute to those decisions.
    </p>

    <p>
      In this unit, we study how simple classification models can be used to automate moderation and how fairness metrics help reveal the costs of false positives and false negatives.
      The goal is to understand the math behind these decisions and the governance trade-offs that shape policy.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="classification-basics">
    <title>Classification Models for Moderation</title>

    <p>
      A moderation system typically classifies content as either <term>acceptable</term> or <term>disallowed</term>.
      Each classification can be correct or incorrect, and the pattern of errors matters for people and groups.
    </p>

    <!-- TODO: Add a short example of a classifier with a decision threshold and a check-it exercise computing confusion matrix entries from counts. -->

    <definition>
      <term>Confusion Matrix</term>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A confusion matrix organizes predictions versus reality for a binary classifier.
        </p>
        <table>
          <title>Confusion Matrix</title>
          <tabular>
            <row>
              <cell></cell>
              <cell>Predicted Acceptable</cell>
              <cell>Predicted Disallowed</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Actually Acceptable</cell>
              <cell>True Negative (TN)</cell>
              <cell>False Positive (FP)</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Actually Disallowed</cell>
              <cell>False Negative (FN)</cell>
              <cell>True Positive (TP)</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      In content moderation, a false positive means acceptable speech is removed, and a false negative means disallowed content is left online.
      Both can cause harm, but the harms are different.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="fairness-metrics">
    <title>Fairness Metrics and Trade-offs</title>

    <definition>
      <term>Precision</term>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Precision measures the fraction of content labeled disallowed that truly belongs in the disallowed category.
        </p>
        <me>
          \text{Precision} = \frac{TP}{TP + FP}.
        </me>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <definition>
      <term>Recall</term>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Recall measures the fraction of truly disallowed content that the system correctly removes.
        </p>
        <me>
          \text{Recall} = \frac{TP}{TP + FN}.
        </me>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
      A classifier can often be tuned to increase precision at the cost of recall, or vice versa.
      Regulators may prefer a conservative system in some contexts, while communities may demand a system that avoids over-removing protected speech.
    </p>

    <definition>
      <term>Demographic Parity</term>
      <statement>
        <p>
          One fairness goal is to ensure that content from different groups is treated similarly by the classifier.
          For example, the rate of content labeled disallowed should be close across groups with different protected attributes.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <definition>
      <term>Equal Opportunity</term>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Equal opportunity requires that the classifier has similar recall across groups, so that truly disallowed content is detected at similar rates for everyone.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <!-- TODO: Add notation for false positive and false negative rates, and include a short exercise comparing precision/recall trade-offs for two thresholds. -->
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="governance-case-studies">
    <title>Case Study: Oversight and Regulation</title>

    <p>
      The Meta Oversight Board and new laws such as the European Union Digital Services Act show that content moderation is not only a technical problem.
      It is also a matter of governance: who sets the rules, who reviews them, and how harms are balanced.
    </p>

    <p>
      A simplified model helps us see the mathematical decisions behind platform governance.
      For example, regulators may ask whether a classifier should prioritize reducing false negatives to protect people from violence, or reducing false positives to protect free expression.
    </p>

    <exercise>
      <title>Balanced Moderation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose a platform uses a classifier to detect disallowed content. For one community, the classifier produces <m>TP = 60</m>, <m>FP = 20</m>, and <m>FN = 40</m>. For another community, it produces <m>TP = 45</m>, <m>FP = 15</m>, and <m>FN = 15</m>.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Compute precision and recall for each community.</p></li>
          <li><p>Which community experiences a higher false-negative rate? Which community experiences a higher false-positive rate?</p></li>
          <li><p>Discuss how these differences might affect public trust and whether a single threshold is fair.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
